## 9. Versioning and reproducibility

Every completed analysis must identify:

- Source IDs.
- Input hashes.
- Source ordering.
- Detector implementation/schema version.
- Measurement and observation schema versions.
- Geographic claim versions.
- Scoring configuration version.
- Candidate inclusion rule.
- Multi-source aggregation rule.
- Result and evidence records.

These values should also be captured in one compact immutable analysis manifest.
The manifest may be structured data stored with the analysis rather than a new
table for every version dimension. Once an analysis reaches a terminal status,
the manifest cannot be silently rewritten.

For failed or timed-out attempts, the manifest preserves all values known at
input and scheduling time and marks values that were never selected as
unavailable. Every attempt still preserves its exact source IDs and observed
content hashes.

Changing any scoring-affecting definition creates a new version.

Historical analyses are not silently recomputed. A reanalysis of old inputs is a
new analysis linked to the original where such a relationship is needed.

The explanation service reads normalized stored evidence and contributions.
Explanations are derived from analysis records. Persistent checkpoints and
rendered explanation snapshots are deferred until a concrete study workflow
requires them; if later persisted, they must reference the exact analysis and
must not become a second source of reasoning truth.

---

## 10. Separation between runtime and durable state

## 10.1 Durable state

The following must survive process restarts when created successfully:

- Profiles.
- Sessions and rounds.
- Sources and availability status.
- Analysis attempts and terminal statuses.
- Measurements, observations, evidence, and raw outputs retained by policy.
- Claim and scoring versions.
- Candidate scores and contributions.
- Checkpoints.
- Player guesses.
- Actual-location revisions.
- Evaluations.
- Persisted insight snapshots.

## 10.2 Runtime-only state

The following may remain temporary:

- In-memory image objects.
- Detector work buffers.
- Processing queues.
- Progress indicators.
- Temporary capture buffers.
- Uncommitted explanation drafts.
- Transient learning calculations.

Runtime state must not be presented as durable study history until persistence
succeeds.

